From 07e1e87ebabe679a0409067df1ccb0219b9a785f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Majid Arasskhani <M.Arasskhani@gosilkroad.net>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 15:33:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/22] fix(budget): reconcile locks the subject before
 summing; period rollover runs on a locked row
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Bug (B-7): reconcile_subject_reserved summed the open held rows first and
took the subject's FOR UPDATE lock second. While `reserve` held that lock
with a new reservation not yet committed, the sum read the pre-reservation
snapshot, waited for the lock, then wrote the stale sum over the counter
the reservation had just increased: a live hold that was no longer counted,
i.e. an overspend by exactly that amount. Demonstrated on PostgreSQL: the
new canary fails on the old code with `assert 0.0 == 2.5`.

Bug (B-33): ensure_budget_period rewrote budget_used_usd and
budget_reserved_usd on the request-scoped User loaded without a lock, so a
month rollover raced with reservations/settlements that update the same
columns under SELECT ... FOR UPDATE in other transactions.

Change:
- reconcile_subject_reserved: lock first, sum second.
- reconcile_drifted_reserved_counters: pg_try_advisory_xact_lock(56023116);
  returns 0 when another instance holds it (leader election is the primary
  guard, this is defence in depth for manual runs / a second deployment).
- ensure_budget_period: on a pending rollover, re-read the user FOR UPDATE
  (populate_existing) and re-check before writing.
- CI canary now also runs test_budget_reconcile_race_postgres.py and
  test_readiness.py.

Tests: tests/test_budget_reconcile_race_postgres.py — reconcile blocks on
the in-flight reservation and lands on the true sum; the drift repair is
single-flight behind the advisory lock.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
